---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const getCode = `git clone https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos
cd lightphotos
cargo build --release --bin lightphotos-mcp`;

const appRun = `python3 tools/cull/cull.py \\
  --mcp target/release/lightphotos-mcp \\
  --folder ~/Pictures/my-shoot`;

const modelSetup = `brew install ollama        # Linux and Windows: ollama.com/download
ollama pull qwen3-vl:32b-instruct
ollama serve               # skip if the Ollama app is already running`;

const modelRun = `python3 tools/cull/cull.py \\
  --mcp target/release/lightphotos-mcp \\
  --folder ~/Pictures/my-shoot \\
  --use-model                # only needed on a Mac`;

const claudeSetup = `claude mcp add lightphotos -- \\
  "$PWD/target/release/lightphotos-mcp" --folder ~/Pictures/my-shoot`;

const claudeRun = `claude -p "$(cat tools/cull/claude-prompt.txt)" \\
  --allowedTools mcp__lightphotos`;
---
<Base
  title="Three ways to let AI cull a shoot — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="Culling is the boring part of photography. I handed it to AI three ways through the LightPhotos MCP server: the app's own score, a local model, and Claude Code. The pros and cons of each, and how to try them yourself."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Three ways to let AI cull a shoot</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          Culling is the boring part of photography. I handed it to AI three
          ways: the app&rsquo;s own score, a model running on my computer,
          and Claude Code. The pros and cons of each, and how to try them
          yourself.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          On a Mac, use the app score: 85 RAW files in 16 seconds, and nothing
          leaves your computer. Not on a Mac and want privacy? A local model
          works, but it is slow and too generous. Claude Code is the easiest
          to set up, but it uploads every photo it looks at.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>The boring part</h2>
      <p>
        After a portrait session I have 85 frames, and most of the first pass
        is mechanical: throw out the soft ones, the blinks, the badly exposed
        ones. Choosing the best of what is left is the fun part, and I want to
        keep that.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I gave the mechanical part to AI. LightPhotos has an
        <a href="/blogs/your-next-power-user-cant-see-the-screen.html">MCP server</a>,
        so any AI can look at the photos and set ratings without opening the
        app. All three approaches do the same thing: score every photo from 0
        to 100, then apply one rule.
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead><tr><th>Score</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Below 40</th><td>Rejected</td></tr>
            <tr><th>40 to 59</th><td>2 stars</td></tr>
            <tr><th>60 and up</th><td>Left alone</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The AI only marks the bad and the weak. The good frames stay unrated,
        so the stars are still mine to give.
      </p>
      <p>The only difference is who gives the score.</p>

      <h2>The three approaches</h2>

      <h3>1. The app score</h3>
      <p>
        LightPhotos scores the photo itself: focus, clipped highlights,
        darkness, noise and closed eyes, plus Apple&rsquo;s on-device model of
        what looks good. It is the same score as the Score All button.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> fast. 85 RAW files in 16 seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> private. Nothing leaves your computer, and there is nothing extra to install.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> consistent. Same photos, same answer, every time. Scores are saved, so running it again takes no time.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> macOS only, because it needs Apple&rsquo;s Vision framework.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> it measures quality, not taste. It cannot tell which of six similar frames has the best expression.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. A local model</h3>
      <p>
        A vision model, <code>qwen3-vl:32b-instruct</code>, runs on your own
        computer through Ollama. It looks at four frames at a time and scores
        each.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> private. The model runs on your computer; no photo is uploaded.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> works on Linux and Windows, where the app score does not exist.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> slow. 28 minutes for 85 frames.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> heavy. A 21 GB download, and it wants 32 GB of memory.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> too generous. It scored 80 of 85 frames 60 or more, even when told to be critical, so it flagged almost nothing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Claude Code</h3>
      <p>
        No script. Claude connects to the MCP server, looks at each photo,
        and sets the ratings itself, following a prompt in plain English.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> easiest to set up: one command and a prompt.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> no big download, and it runs anywhere Claude Code runs.</li>
        <li><strong>Pro:</strong> easy to change. Edit the prompt to cull differently.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> every photo it looks at is uploaded to Anthropic. Not for photos of people who did not agree to that.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> it uses your Claude plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Con:</strong> I did not run it on this shoot, for the reason above.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Which to pick</h3>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th class="lp-compare-pick">App score</th><th>Local model</th><th>Claude Code</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Speed, 85 RAW files</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">16 s</td><td>28 min</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Photos leave your computer</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Runs on</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">macOS</td><td>Linux, Windows, macOS</td><td>Anywhere</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Setup</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">Python 3</td><td>Ollama and a 21 GB model</td><td>Claude Code</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Use it when</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">You are on a Mac</td><td>You are not, and the photos are private</td><td>The photos are fine to upload</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Use a copy of a shoot the first time. Close the folder in LightPhotos
        first, because the MCP server will not write to a folder the app has
        open. Ratings and rejects go where the app keeps them; your photo
        files are never changed.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1: get the code (all three)</h3>
      <p>You need <a href="https://rustup.rs">Rust</a> and Python 3.</p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{getCode}</pre>

      <h3>Step 2a: the app score</h3>
      <pre class="lp-code">{appRun}</pre>

      <h3>Step 2b: a local model</h3>
      <p>Install <a href="https://ollama.com/download">Ollama</a> and the model once:</p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{modelSetup}</pre>
      <p>Then:</p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{modelRun}</pre>

      <h3>Step 2c: Claude Code</h3>
      <p>Connect Claude Code to the folder once:</p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{claudeSetup}</pre>
      <p>
        Then run the prompt from
        <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/blob/main/tools/cull/claude-prompt.txt">claude-prompt.txt</a>,
        or start <code>claude</code> and paste it:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">{claudeRun}</pre>

      <h3>Step 3: review</h3>
      <p>
        Open the folder in LightPhotos and hide the rejects. Glance at the
        2-star frames, then pick your favorites from the rest. That is the
        fun part.
      </p>

      <h2>What happened on my shoot</h2>
      <p>
        85 RAW files from one portrait session, each run on a fresh copy. The
        photos stay on my Mac, so there are none here.
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th class="lp-compare-pick">App score</th><th>Local model</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th>Rejected</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">3</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><th>2 stars</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">25</td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Left alone</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">57</td><td>80</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Time</th><td class="lp-compare-pick">16 s</td><td>28 min</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The two agreed on 55 of 85 frames, but 54 of those were just
        &ldquo;left alone&rdquo; by both. The model left alone all 25 frames
        the app gave 2 stars. Only one frame was rejected by both.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The app score is the one I use.</strong> It is a hundred
        times faster than the local model, and it flagged 28 frames for a
        second look where the model flagged 5.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Letting AI make the whole call did not work.</strong> My first
        version let the local model reject and rate every frame on its own
        judgment. Run twice with small changes, it agreed with itself on only
        45 of 83 frames. A plain score and a fixed rule are boring, and that
        is what you want from the boring part.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>None of them picks the best moment.</strong> They catch blur,
        blinks and bad exposure. Choosing between six frames of the same pose
        is still your job, and the best part of culling anyway.
      </p>
      <p>
        The script and its instructions are in
        <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/tree/main/tools/cull">tools/cull</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/your-next-power-user-cant-see-the-screen.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Your next power user can’t see the screen</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
